---
/**
 * USE CASES — Ecommerce. Promoted from /v3 to /use-cases-ecommerce; indexable.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE DESIGN'S §6 IS CUT ENTIRELY. Ruled 2026-08-06. It was "What our DTC
 * users actually change", opening "Three patterns we see when DTC brands
 * install SourceTrack" and asserting "reallocating ~22% of spend off
 * duplicate-claimed clicks". That asserts an observed DTC customer base with
 * measured outcomes. Production has four sites — two free, one founder test,
 * one localhost — and zero paying customers, so there are no DTC users and the
 * 22% is measurable from nothing.
 *
 * CUT, not rewritten. A rewrite ("three patterns attribution surfaces") keeps
 * the shape of evidence we do not have. This is a worse failure than
 * overstating capability: it fabricates proof.
 *
 * ⚠️ "126 verified conversions" — THE NUMBER IS RESTORED, THE WORD IS NOT.
 *
 * It was dropped on my finding that 126 "is not in homeFixtures.js". That was
 * WRONG, and the way it was wrong is the lesson: 126 is TOTALS.conv in the demo
 * fixture, DERIVED as sum(CHANNELS) rather than written as a literal, so a grep
 * for the string found nothing and I read absence as fabrication. A claim scan
 * that only matches literals cannot see a computed value — recorded in
 * api/tests/v3-claims-allowlist.test.js so the next scan resolves rather than
 * greps. The demo fixture and homeFixtures.js are in fact identical channel for
 * channel, so the design was citing real data.
 *
 * "VERIFIED" IS STILL DROPPED, and not merely as unsupported. main has no
 * concept of a verified conversion — attribution_status resolves to
 * attributed/unattributed, which is whether a source could be traced, not
 * whether anything was checked. Worse, main RESERVES the word: ai-crawler-detect.js
 * defines IP_VERIFIED for reverse-DNS-verified crawler identity and states twice
 * that a weaker signal "must not be reported as verified". Putting it on a
 * marketing page would import a term from a security context that guards it,
 * onto something main never verifies.
 *
 * "126 attributed conversions" was also rejected: it would assert all 126 were
 * attributed, which has not been checked against the fixture. Substituting one
 * unverified qualifier for another is the same error in a quieter word.
 *
 * §3's $84 / $252 arithmetic is KEPT. It is explicitly hypothetical — three
 * platforms each claiming the same single order — and it makes the
 * over-attribution point without asserting anything about a real customer.
 * Platform names are TEXT ONLY, no brand marks (§35.3), same treatment as
 * compare-ga4.
 *
 * ⚠️ Shopify is a WEBHOOK, not an app-store integration. ReportBuilder.jsx:137
 * labels both ecom_orders and ecom_shopify "(Manual webhook)". Nothing on this
 * page implies a one-click install.
 */
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Wrap from "@/layouts/v3/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "@/layouts/v3/SectionHead.astro";
import StatRow from "@/layouts/v3/StatRow.astro";
import "@/styles/v3-tokens.css";
import "@/styles/v3-home.css";
import "@/styles/v4-type.css";
import "@/styles/v4-motion.css";
import "@/styles/v3-pages.css";

const stats = [
  { value: "9", label: "attribution models, all on every plan" },
  { value: "16", label: "AI assistants classified on arrival" },
  { value: "$21,430", label: "order revenue reconciled · demo workspace fixture" },
  // RESTORED 2026-08-06, with the qualifier removed. See the header note.
  { value: "126", label: "conversions · demo workspace fixture" },
  // ⚠️ "4 ecommerce report templates, ready to run" WAS HERE and is dropped —
  // NOT on truth grounds. It is supportable: exactly four PRESET_TEMPLATES carry
  // category: 'Ecommerce' (ecom_orders, ecom_revenue, ecom_aov, ecom_shopify,
  // ReportBuilder.jsx:137), and that was verified from source BEFORE deciding, so
  // the cut is an editorial call rather than a correction dressed as one.
  //
  // It goes because restoring 126 made five cells in a repeat(4, 1fr) grid — four
  // on one row, one orphaned on the next — and a template COUNT is the weakest of
  // the five on a use-case page, where the other four are outcomes. Changing the
  // grid to repeat(5, 1fr) for this page alone was rejected: that is one shared
  // StatRow rendering two ways, the same fragmentation a modifier class would have
  // caused. Four cells everywhere, by construction.
];

// Hypothetical by construction — one order, three platforms each claiming it.
const claims = [
  { who: "Meta Ads", claimed: "$84", note: "Counts the order: someone saw an ad inside its attribution window." },
  { who: "Google Ads", claimed: "$84", note: "Counts the same order, for the same reason, from its own window." },
  { who: "TikTok Ads", claimed: "$84", note: "Also counts it. None of the three is lying; each measures alone." },
  { who: "SourceTrack", claimed: "$84", note: "One order, counted once, then split across the journey by the model you pick." },
];

const channels = [
  { h: "Paid social and paid search", p: "Click IDs and campaign params preserved through the landing page, so the order still knows which campaign earned it." },
  { h: "Organic and referral", p: "Referrer kept rather than discarded, including the affiliate and creator traffic that usually collapses into direct." },
  { h: "AI assistants", p: "Sixteen answer engines classified on arrival — the newest channel, and the one that files as direct in every default report." },
  { h: "Email and SMS", p: "Campaign metadata carried across the session boundary, so a flow-driven repeat order is not credited to direct." },
];
---

<Base
  title="Ecommerce attribution — stop letting ad platforms grade their own homework | SourceTrack"
  description="Meta, Google and TikTok each claim the same order. See what one first-party dataset says actually happened."
>
  <div class="v3">
    {/* 1 — hero */}
    <section class="v3-hero">
      <Wrap>
        <h1>Stop letting Meta and Google grade their own homework.</h1>
        <p class="v3-hero-sub">
          Every ad platform reports the conversions it believes it caused. Add
          them up and you have sold more than you shipped. One first-party
          dataset keeps a single count.
        </p>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 2 — stat band */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--tight">
      <Wrap><StatRow stats={stats} /></Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 3 — soft band, table card. Hypothetical arithmetic, not a customer. */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--soft">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Over-attribution"
          heading="Three platforms. One sale."
          lede="Take a single $84 order. If you add up what each platform reports, you sold $252 of product. You sold $84. This is the arithmetic every DTC team quietly lives with."
        />
        <div class="v3-table-card">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Reported by</th><th>Claims</th><th>What it is counting</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {claims.map((c) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{c.who}</th><td>{c.claimed}</td><td>{c.note}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 4 — paper, channel cards */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="DTC channel mix"
          heading="Every channel a DTC team actually uses."
          lede="Channel classification runs on arrival, from one shared list — so a new AI assistant is recognised the same way a paid-search click is, without a per-report rule."
        />
        <div class="v3-cards">
          {channels.map((c) => (
            <div class="v3-card"><h3>{c.h}</h3><p>{c.p}</p></div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 5 — soft band, compare split. Claims about the Shopify default report are
        limited to what it does by default, the same discipline compare-ga4 uses
        for GA4. Shopify support here is a webhook, and says so. */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--soft">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Alongside Shopify"
          heading="Better than the default traffic report."
          lede="Shopify's own report is fine for sessions. It was never built to explain why those sessions became orders, or which of three platforms deserves the credit."
        />
        <div class="v3-compare-split">
          <div class="v3-compare-panel">
            <h3>Default traffic report</h3>
            <p class="v3-compare-value">Sessions by source</p>
            <p>Counts visits. Untagged and referrer-stripped traffic collapses into direct, and one order can still be claimed by three ad platforms at once.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-compare-panel">
            <h3>SourceTrack</h3>
            <p class="v3-compare-value">Revenue by source</p>
            <p>Orders arrive through a Shopify or Stripe webhook, tied to the journey that produced them, then credited under whichever of the nine models you pick.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 6 — the design's "What our DTC users actually change". CUT. Header note. */}

    {/* 7 — CTA close on paper. Lime on the button only (§2.6). */}
    <section class="v3-cta-close">
      <Wrap>
        <div class="v3-cta-inner">
          <div>
            <h2>Run alongside Shopify. Run alongside Meta. Be the referee.</h2>
            <p>28 days free, no card. Install the script, connect the order webhook, and see which channel actually earned the sale.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-cta-actions">
            <a class="v3-btn-accent" href="https://app.sourcetrack.ai/signup">Start 28-day free trial</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="v3-disclosure">Product visuals shown use illustrative data.</p>
      </Wrap>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
